<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayavarman II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/802">802 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayavarman II was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> ruler who in 802 CE proclaimed himself universal monarch, or chakravartin, in a ceremony declaring independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. His reign is traditionally regarded as the foundation of the Khmer Empire. He established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a> cult of kingship.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Few firm facts survive about Jayavarman II. No inscriptions written by him have been found, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> kings remembered him as a warrior and the most powerful king of that time. Before he came to power the local overlords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> were in constant warfare, and much of the area fell within the orbit of the Shailendra dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>. According to one account he spent his childhood in captivity or exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and was installed as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> around 800. The Baksei Chamkrong inscription of 947 makes him a descendant of Rudravarman and of the semi-mythical Kaundinya and Soma.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jayavarman II is widely regarded as the king who set the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> period, beginning with the consecration ritual of 802 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mahendraparvata">Mount Mahendraparvata</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-kulen">Phnom Kulen</a>. The Sdok Kak Thom inscription, dated to 1052, two centuries after his death, says the Brahmin Hiranyadama and the chief priest Sivakaivalya performed a rite that released the kingdom from subjection to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and consecrated him as a chakravartin, lord of the universe. It also records the creation of the cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">devaraja</a>.</p><p>Records suggest that he and his followers moved over some years from southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to the northwest, subduing principalities on the way. He founded Hariharalaya near present-day Roluos. No temple is associated with him, but some historians suggest he built Ak Yum, a ruined brick stepped pyramid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jayavarman II died in 850 and received the posthumous name Parameshwara, supreme ruler. His son Jayavarman III followed him, and he was honoured in the Preah Ko temple at Roluos, inaugurated in 880. Scholars disagree about what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> means in the inscription: Coedès and Briggs took it for the island, Charles Higham doubts that, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-vickery">Michael Vickery</a> reads it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a>, and Takashi Suzuki places it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. Historians also debate whether his rule was a turning point or part of a long process. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a> saw Angkorian achievements as proof of the greatness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> people, and the flag of Democratic Kampuchea, adopted on 5 January 1976, bore a three-towered temple representing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jayavarman II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sdok Kak Thom inscription of 1053 records that <b>Jayavarman II</b> and his son Indrayudha defeated a Cham army in 790 before moving north of the Tonlé Sap to found Hariharalaya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundation is traditionally dated to 802 CE, when <b>Jayavarman II</b> underwent a consecration ritual on Mount Mahendraparvata, the Phnom Kulen plateau, proclaiming himself a universal monarch (chakravartin), inaugurating the cult of the devaraja and symbolically freeing the land from the overlordship of “Java”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Khmer prince <b>Jayavarman II</b> spent years at the Shailendra court before returning around 790 to rule as devaraja and proclaim independence from Java in 802, founding the Angkor era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Since <b>Jayavarman II</b> proclaimed himself chakravartin, “universal ruler”, and devaraja, “god-king”, in 802, Khmer kings were held to have the divine quality of living gods, incarnations of Shiva or Vishnu.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Its territories were absorbed into the Khmer Empire after <b>Jayavarman II</b> proclaimed his rule in 802, and many historians doubt that the Chinese division matched clear political boundaries.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-chenla">Land Chenla</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jayavarman II</b>, who had spent time in Java, returned to unite the Khmer lands and in 802 proclaimed himself universal monarch, ending the division and founding the Khmer Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/water-chenla">Water Chenla</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.43,103.83&amp;year=802" aria-label="Angkor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7396 1663 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jayavarman II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=13.43,103.83&amp;year=802">What was here in 802 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
